 'title'=>'Web Development Foundations','slug'=>'web-development-foundations','category'=>'web-development','description'=>'Turn a blank browser into something you built. Learn the essentials of HTML, CSS and responsive design, one practical step at a time.','objectives'=>"Understand how the web works\nBuild accessible pages with semantic HTML\nStyle responsive layouts with CSS\nPublish a personal profile project",
 'stages'=>[
  ['title'=>'Meet the web','description'=>'Get comfortable with the building blocks behind every website.','lessons'=>[
   ['title'=>'How the web works','minutes'=>10,'description'=>'Follow a web page from an address in your browser to the screen in front of you.','content'=>"## A conversation between two computers\nWhen you enter a web address, your browser asks a server for a resource. A server is a computer that responds to requests. The browser receives files and turns them into the page you can read and interact with.\n\nA domain name is a human-friendly address. DNS helps translate that name into the server’s network address. HTTPS protects the connection between your browser and the server; it does not guarantee that the content of a site is trustworthy.\n\n## Three building blocks\nHTML gives a page its meaning and structure: headings, paragraphs, links and forms. CSS controls presentation: colour, spacing and layout. JavaScript adds behaviour: opening a menu, validating a form or updating content without a full page load.\n\n## Try it yourself\nOpen a familiar website in your browser. Use the browser menu to open Developer Tools, then find the Elements panel. Inspect a heading and a paragraph. You are looking at the document structure that the browser uses. Temporary changes here affect only your local view, not the server.\n\nNext, open the Network panel and reload the page. Notice that a single page can request several files. Find the main document and inspect its status code. A 200 response usually means the request succeeded; 404 means the resource was not found.\n\n## Pause and explain\nIn your own words, describe the roles of a browser, a server, HTML and CSS. Write your explanation in your private notes. Being able to explain a concept simply is a useful check on your understanding.",'takeaways'=>"A browser requests resources from a server.\nHTML is structure; CSS is presentation; JavaScript is behaviour.\nDeveloper Tools let you inspect your local view of a page.",'checklist'=>['Inspect a heading in Developer Tools.','Explain the browser–server relationship in your notes.']],
   ['title'=>'Your first HTML page','minutes'=>15,'description'=>'Create a small, meaningful page using headings, paragraphs and links.','content'=>"## Start with a document\nCreate a folder for your practice work and a file named index.html. Open the folder in a text editor. Add the structure below, save it, then open the file in your browser.\n\n```html\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n  <title>My learning journal</title>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <main>\n    <h1>My learning journal</h1>\n    <p>I am learning to build useful things for the web.</p>\n  </main>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n## Choose meaning before appearance\nUse one clear main heading for the page. Use h2 elements for major sections and h3 for subsections. Choose heading levels because of document structure, not because of their default font sizes. A paragraph belongs in a p element. A list of related items belongs in ul or ol.\n\nA link takes someone to another place. A button performs an action. This distinction helps people using keyboards or assistive technology understand what will happen.\n\n## Make a useful page\nAdd an About me section, a list of three skills you want to learn, and a link to a resource you trust. Give the link descriptive text such as Read the HTML guide rather than Click here. If you add an image, write useful alternative text or use an empty alt value when the image is purely decorative.\n\n## Test your structure\nReload after each change. Move through your links using the Tab key. Check that the browser title is useful and that the content still makes sense without any styling. Save a copy of this page: you will improve it in the next stage.",'takeaways'=>"Semantic HTML communicates the meaning of content.\nHeadings should form a logical hierarchy.\nLinks navigate; buttons perform actions.",'checklist'=>['Create and open your index.html file.','Add an About me section and a list of learning goals.','Navigate the page using only your keyboard.']]
  ],'quiz'=>'HTML foundations'],
  ['title'=>'Make it your own','description'=>'Give your page a visual identity, then make it work on every screen.','lessons'=>[
   ['title'=>'Style with intention','minutes'=>15,'description'=>'Use CSS to create a clear, readable visual hierarchy.','content'=>"## Connect a stylesheet\nCreate a file named styles.css beside your HTML file. Inside the document head, add a link element with rel set to stylesheet and href set to styles.css. Keeping presentation in a stylesheet makes it easier to change and reuse.\n\n```css\nbody {\n  margin: 0;\n  font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;\n  line-height: 1.6;\n  color: #191919;\n  background: #fff9f5;\n}\nmain {\n  max-width: 65ch;\n  margin: 0 auto;\n  padding: 2rem 1rem;\n}\na { color: #b33e10; }\n```\n\n## Understand the box model\nEvery element occupies a box. Content sits inside padding. A border surrounds that padding, and margin separates the box from its neighbours. Setting box-sizing to border-box makes an element’s declared width include its padding and border.\n\n## Build a visual hierarchy\nUse a small, repeatable spacing scale, such as 8, 16, 24 and 32 pixels. Make the main heading distinct, keep body text comfortably readable and limit the number of accent colours. A consistent page usually feels more intentional than a busy one.\n\n## Practise\nStyle your learning journal. Give the main content a comfortable maximum width. Add space between sections. Choose one accent colour for links and buttons. Keep a visible focus style so keyboard users can see where they are.\n\nFinally, temporarily disable your stylesheet. The page should still have a sensible structure. Styling enhances meaning; it does not replace it.",'takeaways'=>"The box model describes content, padding, border and margin.\nA consistent spacing scale makes a page easier to scan.\nVisible keyboard focus is part of good visual design.",'checklist'=>['Create and connect a stylesheet.','Add consistent spacing and a readable content width.','Keep a visible focus style for links.']],
   ['title'=>'Layouts that adapt','minutes'=>15,'description'=>'Make your page feel at home on a phone, tablet and laptop.','content'=>"## Start small\nResponsive design lets a layout adapt to the space available. Start with a single-column layout that works well on a narrow screen. Add complexity only when the content has enough room.\n\n## Use flexible tools\nFlexbox is useful for aligning items in a row or column. CSS Grid is useful for two-dimensional layouts. Avoid fixed widths that force a page to scroll sideways.\n\n```css\n.skills {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr));\n  gap: 1rem;\n}\nimg {\n  max-width: 100%;\n  height: auto;\n}\n```\n\n## Choose breakpoints from your content\nA breakpoint is a condition where a design changes. Add one when your content needs it, not just because a device category exists. For example, a navigation row may become too crowded below a certain width.\n\n## Test, don’t guess\nResize your browser slowly. Use its responsive mode to check narrow widths, but also test keyboard navigation and text zoom. Look for clipped headings, tiny tap targets and horizontal overflow.\n\n## Practice task\nTurn your skill list into flexible cards. At a narrow width, the cards should stack. At a wider width, they can share a row. Make sure the reading order remains meaningful in the HTML.",'takeaways'=>"Start with a usable narrow-screen layout.\nFlexible sizing is more resilient than fixed widths.\nTest text zoom and keyboard access as well as screen sizes.",'checklist'=>['Create a flexible skills-card layout.','Test at a narrow width and at 200% zoom.','Fix any horizontal overflow.']]
