  ['title'=>'Build & share','description'=>'Bring the pieces together in a small project with a purpose.','lessons'=>[
   ['title'=>'A practical accessibility check','minutes'=>10,'description'=>'Make sure more people can use what you build.','content'=>"## Accessibility is part of quality\nA useful website should not depend on one kind of device, input method or ability. Accessibility starts with semantic HTML and continues through design, testing and feedback.\n\n## Check the basics\nUse your keyboard to move through the page. Every interactive control should be reachable, and focus should be visible. The focus order should follow the reading order. Do not remove focus outlines without providing an equivalent.\n\nRead the page headings as an outline. Each should describe the section that follows. Give form inputs visible labels, and explain errors in text rather than using colour alone.\n\nCheck colour contrast with a reputable contrast checker. As a baseline, normal text needs at least 4.5:1 contrast and large text at least 3:1 under WCAG AA. Strong contrast helps in bright light as well as for people with low vision.\n\n## Be careful with motion\nAvoid unnecessary movement. Respect the prefers-reduced-motion media query when adding animation. Moving content should not get in the way of reading or completing a task.\n\n## Practice task\nWrite a short accessibility checklist for your profile page. Test it with a keyboard and at 200% text zoom. Record one issue you found and how you fixed it.",'takeaways'=>"Semantic structure and visible labels reduce barriers.\nTest real keyboard interactions.\nAccessibility improves through repeated testing, not a single score.",'checklist'=>['Test keyboard navigation and visible focus.','Check text contrast and zoom.','Record an issue and your fix.']],
   ['title'=>'Give your project a final polish','minutes'=>15,'description'=>'Review your profile page, explain your choices and prepare to share it.','content'=>"## Make the purpose clear\nA portfolio project is more useful when someone can understand what it does and why you built it. Give your profile page a clear introduction, a small skills section and a way to explore your work. You do not need a complicated design.\n\n## Review before sharing\nCheck spelling, link destinations and image descriptions. Open the page at a narrow width and a wide width. Verify that buttons and links behave as expected. Remove placeholder content and anything you do not have permission to publish.\n\n## Document your decisions\nWrite a short project description: the problem, your approach, the tools you used and one thing you learned. Be honest about work that remains. A clear explanation often communicates more than a long list of technologies.\n\n## Share safely\nIf you publish your project with a hosting provider, follow that provider’s current instructions. Do not upload passwords, private keys or other secrets. A public repository is public: review its files and history before sharing it.\n\n## Your next step\nOpen the profile-page assignment in this stage. Submit a project link, a repository URL or an allowed file, together with a short explanation. Your reviewer will use the published rubric. A submitted project is not yet an approved project; read feedback and make revisions when requested.",'takeaways'=>"Explain your project’s purpose and design decisions.\nReview content and interactions before publishing.\nKeep secrets and private information out of public projects.",'checklist'=>['Review your page on mobile and desktop widths.','Write a short project explanation.','Check that shared files contain no secrets.']]
