 'title'=>'UI/UX Design Essentials','slug'=>'ui-ux-design-essentials','category'=>'ui-ux-design','description'=>'Design with people in mind. Turn a real user need into clear flows, thoughtful wireframes and interfaces that make sense.','objectives'=>"Frame a user problem\nPlan a clear user journey\nSketch and test a wireframe\nExplain a design decision",
 'stages'=>[
  ['title'=>'Start with people','description'=>'Understand the problem before you design the screen.','lessons'=>[
   ['title'=>'A useful problem comes first','minutes'=>10,'description'=>'Separate a real user need from a feature idea.','content'=>"## Start with a situation\nA useful design begins with an understanding of what someone is trying to do. A feature idea, such as adding a dashboard, is not yet a problem statement. Ask who needs help, in what situation and what gets in their way.\n\n## Observe before deciding\nTalk to someone who performs the task. Ask them to describe a recent experience rather than predict what they might do. Use open questions such as What happened next? and What made that difficult? Ask permission before recording and avoid collecting unnecessary personal data.\n\n## Write a focused problem statement\nTry this structure: A person in a particular situation needs a way to achieve an outcome because of a specific barrier. Keep the statement neutral about the solution.\n\n## Practice\nChoose a small everyday task, such as finding a bus departure or planning a study session. Write down your assumptions. Speak with one willing person about their experience, then separate what you observed from what you inferred. One conversation is a starting point, not proof that everyone shares the same need.\n\nWrite a problem statement and one question you still need to investigate.",'takeaways'=>"A user problem describes a need, not a feature.\nSeparate observations from assumptions.\nResearch requires consent and care with personal information.",'checklist'=>['Choose one small everyday task.','Write a neutral problem statement.','List one assumption to investigate.']],
   ['title'=>'Map the simplest useful journey','minutes'=>15,'description'=>'Turn a goal into a small, understandable sequence of steps.','content'=>"## A journey has a goal\nWrite the starting situation and the outcome the user wants. List only the steps needed to move between them. A useful flow shows actions, decisions and possible failure states.\n\n## Make decisions visible\nFor a study planner, a simple flow could be: choose a goal, select study days, choose a reminder time, review the plan and save it. Ask what happens if no days are selected or reminders are unavailable.\n\n## Reduce unnecessary work\nDo not ask for information before it is needed. Use defaults carefully, make them visible and let people change them. Explain why sensitive information is requested.\n\n## Practice\nSketch your flow on paper. Use rectangles for screens and arrows for transitions. Add one alternative route and one error state. Then explain the flow to another person without defending it. Note where they hesitate.\n\nRevise the flow so each step has one clear purpose. Keep your first version small enough to test.",'takeaways'=>"A flow connects a starting situation to a desired outcome.\nError states and alternatives are part of the design.\nA small testable flow is better than an untested complex one.",'checklist'=>['Sketch a task flow with a clear outcome.','Include an alternative route and an error state.','Revise one confusing step.']]
  ]],
  ['title'=>'Make the idea tangible','description'=>'Create a wireframe, test the task and explain what you learned.','lessons'=>[
   ['title'=>'Wireframes before decoration','minutes'=>15,'description'=>'Use a low-fidelity sketch to explore structure and hierarchy.','content'=>"## Make structure cheap to change\nA wireframe is a simple representation of a screen. Its purpose is to explore content, hierarchy and interaction before investing in visual polish. Paper is enough to begin.\n\n## Design around the task\nGive each screen a clear main action. Group related information. Use descriptive labels instead of vague ones such as Submit when a more useful label, such as Save study plan, is available.\n\n## Add real words\nPlaceholder text hides content problems. Write realistic headings, field labels, helper text and error messages. Keep the language short, specific and respectful.\n\n## Practice\nSketch three screens for your task flow. Mark the primary action on each. Include one empty state and one validation message. Check whether someone can understand the task from the screen titles and action labels alone.\n\nDo not confuse a clean-looking wireframe with a validated solution. The next step is to observe someone trying to use it.",'takeaways'=>"Wireframes explore structure before visual polish.\nReal interface copy exposes design problems.\nEach screen needs a clear purpose and action.",'checklist'=>['Sketch three related screens.','Write meaningful labels and one error message.','Identify the primary action on each screen.']],
   ['title'=>'Test, listen and iterate','minutes'=>15,'description'=>'Learn from a small usability test without leading the participant.','content'=>"## Give a task, not instructions\nAsk a willing participant to achieve an outcome with your wireframe. For example: You want to study on three evenings each week. Set up a plan. Do not tell them which controls to use.\n\n## Observe with care\nExplain that you are testing the design, not the person. Ask permission to take notes. Watch what they do and listen for uncertainty. Avoid explaining the interface while they are trying the task.\n\n## Organise what you learned\nSeparate observations, possible interpretations and proposed changes. A participant overlooking a button is an observation. The button is too small is a hypothesis you can test.\n\n## Improve one thing\nPrioritise a change that removes a clear obstacle. Update the wireframe and try the task again. A small informal test can reveal issues, but it cannot establish a statistically representative success rate.\n\n## Practice\nWrite a short design case study: problem, flow, wireframe, test observations and one revision. Add it to your Learnow portfolio as your own work. Choose public visibility only when you have removed private research information and have permission to share any participant material.",'takeaways'=>"A usability task should describe an outcome.\nObserve behaviour without coaching the participant.\nExplain your revision with evidence, not invented metrics.",'checklist'=>['Write an outcome-based test task.','Record observations separately from interpretations.','Make and explain one design revision.']]
  ]]
 ]
