 'title'=>'Data Analysis Fundamentals','slug'=>'data-analysis-fundamentals','category'=>'data-analysis','description'=>'Find the story in a spreadsheet. Ask better questions, clean a small dataset and turn your findings into a clear, honest chart.','objectives'=>"Define an answerable data question\nInspect and clean a small dataset\nChoose useful summary measures\nCommunicate findings without misleading claims",
 'stages'=>[
  ['title'=>'Ask a better question','description'=>'Give your analysis a purpose and understand your source.','lessons'=>[
   ['title'=>'From curiosity to a data question','minutes'=>10,'description'=>'Define what you want to know before opening a spreadsheet.','content'=>"## Begin with a decision\nUseful analysis helps answer a question or inform a decision. Instead of exploring everything, write one focused question. For example: Which weekday had the most completed study sessions during a particular month?\n\n## Define the terms\nWhat counts as a completed session? Which month and timezone apply? Is the dataset complete? Writing definitions early prevents you from changing the rules to suit a result.\n\n## Know the source\nRecord where the data came from, what each row represents and what each column means. Check whether you have permission to use it. Do not copy private learner or customer data into a public practice project.\n\n## Practice\nCreate a small table from your own non-sensitive study log, or use a clearly labelled synthetic practice dataset. Include a date, an activity type and a completion status. If you create synthetic data, never present its results as real-world outcomes.\n\nWrite one question your table can answer and one question it cannot answer.",'takeaways'=>"Define the question, timeframe and terms before analysing.\nRecord the source and meaning of each field.\nSynthetic data must be clearly labelled as synthetic.",'checklist'=>['Write one answerable data question.','Describe what one row represents.','Document the source and any limitations.']],
   ['title'=>'Clean data without losing the story','minutes'=>15,'description'=>'Find duplicates, missing values and inconsistent formats.','content'=>"## Preserve the original\nKeep an untouched copy of your source file. Work on a separate copy and record important cleaning decisions. Reproducible work is easier to review and correct.\n\n## Inspect each column\nLook for inconsistent date formats, extra spaces, unexpected categories and missing values. A blank value is not automatically zero. Zero means a measured quantity of none; a blank may mean the value was not recorded.\n\n## Check duplicates carefully\nTwo identical-looking rows might be an accidental duplicate, or they might describe two real events. Use a stable identifier where available and understand the source before deleting records.\n\n## Practice\nReview your practice table. Standardise activity labels and dates. Count missing values. Create a short cleaning log that records each change, the reason and the number of affected rows.\n\nDo not quietly remove inconvenient values. If a row is excluded, explain why and consider how that decision changes the result.",'takeaways'=>"Keep a raw source copy.\nMissing values and zero are not interchangeable.\nCleaning decisions should be documented.",'checklist'=>['Preserve an original copy of the table.','Inspect missing values and duplicate rows.','Write a short cleaning log.']]
  ]],
  ['title'=>'Find and explain a pattern','description'=>'Summarise carefully and communicate clearly.','lessons'=>[
   ['title'=>'Summaries that answer the question','minutes'=>15,'description'=>'Choose counts, percentages and averages with care.','content'=>"## Match the measure to the question\nA count tells you how many. A percentage compares a part with a defined whole. A mean adds values and divides by their count; a median is the middle value after sorting. These measures answer different questions.\n\n## Name your denominator\nIf you say 60 percent of sessions were completed, explain which sessions are included. Are cancelled sessions counted? Is the timeframe consistent? A percentage without a clear denominator can mislead.\n\n## Look at the distribution\nA single average can hide variation. Compare a mean with a median, inspect the minimum and maximum, and look for unusual values. Do not assume that an outlier is an error.\n\n## Practice\nSummarise your study log by weekday. Count scheduled and completed sessions separately, then calculate a completion percentage only where the scheduled count is greater than zero.\n\nWrite one sentence describing the result and one sentence describing a limitation. A small personal dataset does not justify claims about all learners.",'takeaways'=>"Choose a measure that matches the question.\nAlways explain a percentage’s denominator.\nA summary is more useful with its limitations.",'checklist'=>['Create a weekday summary.','Name the denominator of any percentage.','Write one limitation of the result.']],
   ['title'=>'Make an honest chart','minutes'=>15,'description'=>'Use a simple chart to make a result easier to understand.','content'=>"## Choose a chart for the comparison\nA bar chart compares categories. A line chart can show change over an ordered time sequence. A scatter plot can reveal relationships between two numerical variables. Avoid decorative complexity when a simple chart answers the question.\n\n## Label what matters\nUse a descriptive title, readable labels and clear units. A bar chart should usually start its value axis at zero because bar length represents magnitude. If an axis is truncated in another chart type, make that choice clear.\n\n## Keep the claim proportional\nA pattern in a chart is not proof of causation. A rise in two measures at the same time does not establish that one caused the other. Consider confounding factors, missing data and selection effects.\n\n## Practice\nCreate a bar chart of completed study sessions by weekday. Add the timeframe and source. Use a consistent colour unless a specific comparison needs emphasis. Check that the chart is understandable without relying on colour alone.\n\nPrepare a small portfolio project containing your question, source notes, cleaning log, summary and chart. Remove private information before choosing public visibility. If the data is synthetic, label the chart and project clearly.",'takeaways'=>"Choose a chart that fits the comparison.\nUse honest scales and clear source notes.\nCorrelation alone does not establish causation.",'checklist'=>['Create and label a simple chart.','Include source, timeframe and limitations.','Review the chart for misleading scales or claims.']]
  ]]
 ]
